\section{What counts as a website}{

The fields are free text, and much of it is not a website: \code{N/A}, \code{NONE},
\code{TBD}, \verb{SEE SCHEDULE O}, \verb{UNDER CONSTRUCTION}, an organisation's name, even
\code{xxx-xx-xxxx}. Rather than list placeholders, a value is kept only if, after
cleaning, its host is a valid domain name: dot-separated labels of letters,
digits and hyphens ending in a 2-24 letter top-level domain. Cleaning
lowercases, turns a comma into a dot (\verb{www,ptk.org} is a common typo), and
strips the scheme, a leading \code{www.} and any port. A value with spaces in its
host is not a website.

An email address (\code{info@example.org}) is kept by its domain, as \code{kind = 'email'}, because an organisation's mail domain is usually its web domain.
Mail from a personal provider (gmail.com, yahoo.com, ...) is dropped: it says
nothing about which organisation sent it.
}

\section{\code{site_key}}{
 what a match would compare:
For most sites \code{site_key} is the host without \code{www.}. For pages hosted on a
shared platform (facebook.com, sites.google.com, a \verb{*.wixsite.com} page,
linktr.ee, ...) the host identifies the platform, not the organisation, so
the key is the host plus the first path segment (\code{facebook.com/hullscove}),
and \code{is_platform} is \code{TRUE}. A bare platform host with no path is dropped.

\code{n_eins_sharing} counts the EINs holding each \code{site_key}, across all sources.
A national body's site written by thousands of chapters (\code{elks.org}) is
family evidence, not identity evidence - the same distinction \code{name_compact}
draws with its own \code{n_eins_sharing}.
}

\section{Only known entities}{

A website needs an entity to belong to. Rows for EINs absent from \code{entity}
are held back and counted, not admitted: the e-Postcard has 73,503 such EINs,
and whether to admit them is a decision for an e-Postcard entity ingest, not
